meta {
  name: Refresh - replay after the grace window
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "refresh_token": "{{spentRefreshToken}}"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // The eleven seconds this collection is slower than it looks, and they are
  // the point: reuse detection is the security property the whole rotation
  // scheme exists for, and it cannot be observed over HTTP without letting the
  // grace window close. RSpec travels through time instead
  // (spec/services/auth/refresh_tokens_spec.rb); a live collection has to wait.
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 11000));
}

tests {
  test("detects the reuse and ends the whole family", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
    expect(res.body.error.code).to.equal("token_reuse_detected");
  });
}

docs {
  Family-based reuse detection, once the grace window has closed.

  Outside those ten seconds, a token presented twice is a token somebody kept —
  taken from a log, a backup, or a proxy — and there is no way to tell whose
  copy is whose. So the entire family is revoked and everyone holding one of its
  tokens signs in again.

  The code is distinct from `invalid_refresh_token` because the situations are:
  this one means a token that *was* valid has been presented after it was spent.

  After this runs, every token in the family is dead, including the successors
  the two requests above issued. `accessToken` keeps working until it expires —
  the fifteen-minute window research.md D10 records — so `02 - Links` still
  runs. Re-run `Sign in` to get a working refresh token back.
}
